this is tamar hussein reading for  reviewer alessandro filari  watchdog's legion takes the foundations  and ideas of its predecessors and  expands upon them  exponentially the core conceit of legion  lies in the old adage of strength in  numbers  which manifests by letting you recruit  and play as nearly any character you  come across in the game  this open-ended stance to fighting the  system is a significant change for the  franchise and it's bolstered by improved  hacking and social engineering gameplay  legion's approach while admirable does  have some unintended issues that makes  its powerful message of unity waver in  opportune times  but it still manages to make a profound  statement about hope  legion is all about bringing  long-standing hacker group dedsec  out from the shadows and mobilizing the  citizens of london  against an authoritarian regime london  and its citizens are effectively caught  in a vice grip of encroaching fascism  due to the occupation of albion a  private military group along with  criminal and corporate enterprises  taking advantage of the power vacuum  the london and watchdogs legion is  presented as a more advanced and  exaggerated version of the real life  london however this interpretation of  the city still reflects the present mood  of 2020 albeit with a more cyberpunk  aesthetic  in a game with multiple characters the  one constant that you'll have across  them is your connection to london itself  the game manages to capture the history  and cultural diversity of london while  also juxtaposing it with the dark  presence of albion  who detains citizens in plain sight  [Music]  we could use that power for good the  main story of legion is heavy in its  subject matter  it grapples with themes around  nationalism capitalism classism  and of course policing although the  execution is somewhat scatter shot  the moments in which the game chooses to  shine a light on these pervading issues  can be sobering  the most notable example involves a  guest appearance by british gram rapper  stormzy  who gives a rousing and powerful  performance confronting systemic racism  through text messages podcasts and other  supplementary means of delivering  narrative  legion lays bare even more of the issues  plaguing society  text messages reveal that albion  effectively a police force is targeting  and harassing citizens in public  and recalling this information as you  watch people on the streets fall victim  to these practices can sting  radio shows mock the in-game right-wing  ideologies which  when described sound ridiculous and the  stuff of fiction  but aren't all that far from what we  hear in real life  the people claiming their medical  shortages are probably high on  lots of medicine that they've been  i for one believe that there are no  medical shortages andy just last week i  went in with a broken arm and they  suggested it might just be extreme  period pain and described me a drink of  water in an astrology chart  what really hits home about watchdog's  legion is that as the cartoonishly bleak  world comes into focus  it becomes difficult to ignore the  immediate parallels to our own real  world society  sure it might not interrogate the issues  and explore the nuances with a great  deal of depth  but the broad strokes of the brush are  enough to blur the line between fiction  and reality  and at that point you have to confront  just how far our own society has fallen  for the most part the main narrative  does a decent job of tackling these  topics but its focus is  scatter shot and the game's scope is too  broad to deliver its message in a sharp  concise way as a result the 25 hour main  plot can feel like it drifts into the  periphery as the core narrative becomes  secondary to the moment to moment  actions  and the events that occur when the open  world mechanics are at work  the knock-on effect is that side  activities are left to do  a lot of the heavy lifting on exploring  the nuance in these themes which they do  admirably despite some issues with tonal  inconsistency that come from the open  ended character selection this can lead  to some unexpected and bizarre shifts in  tone with some of the more colorful  characters sticking out uncomfortably  during more serious parts of the story  so this is what this country does to  people with wrong stamps on their  passports  cool so cool so glad that i live here  despite its dark story playing legion  can still often be a light-hearted  experience  and the game has plenty of side stories  that let you use your operatives and  over-the-top  hacking powers to bend the environment  to your whim once you begin to liberate  london and take on higher stakes  missions  you'll eventually unlock more skilled  and less subtle operatives such as spies  with access to cars outfitted with  cloaking and rockets  and drone engineers who can summon robot  bees to swarm enemies like previous  games  watchdog's legion hinges on the familiar  loop of open mode gameplay which entails  exploration  combat stealth and in-game narrative  events watchdog's legion reaches its  high when it's channeling elements of  immersive sims  where many of your choices can cascade  into a satisfying chain  of reactions that yield the desired  result hacking is your core connection  to the world  and it opens up many clever  opportunities when it comes to figuring  out how to achieve your present goals  and it's always satisfying coming up  with ways to bend the environment to  complete your tasks with minimal  resistance  coupled with the randomly generated  playable characters legion presents many  interesting opportunities to take  advantage of and its core gameplay  represents the prospect of the watchdogs  hacker fantasy at its best  it can be bizarre seeing what sort of  characters you pull in on your crusade  and it's solid fun getting used to  whatever skills they have  in some critical missions to succeed by  the skin of their teeth  while each operative is capable missions  can and will likely turn  sour after a wrong decision resulting in  all sorts of enemies coming down on you  with assault rifles and combat drones  the potential addition to this stress is  the option to enhance the campaign to  include permanent deaths for your  operatives meaning once they fall in a  mission  they're gone for good it's a clever  addition that adds a bit more realism  and a greater sense of consequence to  the campaign which can make some of the  more daring missions feel more  stressful especially knowing that losing  a favorite operative will leave a  lasting sting  legion leans heavily into randomly  generated content when recruiting new  operatives and seeing where their story  takes you  over time however it can be easy to fall  into a familiar and repetitive loop of  recycled objectives and dialogues set in  all too familiar areas  albeit with different voiceovers as  engaging as it is to constantly bounce  around characters  over time legion's many wacky excursions  around london can give rise to periods  of aimlessness  without a centralized viewpoint and  structure to guide you in moments like  this the overall narrative  and the tonal issues that watchdog's  legion has can  force their way to the front of the mind  but the core gameplay of hacking the  world  making connections and seeing your  favorite operative succeed is satisfying  enough that it makes it easier not to  get hung up on them  in the past ubisoft has had an  unfortunate history of missing the mark  when it comes to utilizing real world  events  politics and other hot button issues as  the backdrop for their games  however watchdog's legion does a lot to  confront topics like fascism inequality  and the idea that technology exposes and  amplifies old fears and hatreds  legion not only does an effective job of  showing the banality of evil in plain  sight but also  in a more optimistic light shows how the  citizens look to london's culture and to  each other to overcome oppression  watchdog's legion is an anti-fascist  game and it's admirable that it sticks  to this message and sees it through to a  satisfying and affirming conclusion  it also bolsters the franchise's clever  hacking gameplay to offer more  creativity than ever  one of legion's more profound messages  is about what it means to be a true  londoner and by the game's end you'll  have a deadset crew made out of wildly  diverse  and disparate citizens from unique  cultural ethnic  and economic backgrounds all united in  their goal to restore their home  if anything that's as powerful a message  for the game as you can get  think i'm getting this dead sex thing  you wouldn't have been recruited if  deadset thought you'd be killed that  easily  right you're running some cheeky beta  program bagley  don't let bagley get under your skin job  well done

